menu "DeskEmoji cloud device channel"

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_ENABLE
        bool "Enable the self-hosted console channel"
        default n
        help
            When enabled the device periodically contacts your own server to
            fetch emotion, action, volume and announcement commands issued from
            the console, and reports its firmware version and volume.

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_URL
        string "Server base URL"
        depends on ROBOT_CLOUD_ENABLE
        default ""
        help
            No trailing slash.  Use https://your-domain in production, or
            http://<pc-lan-address>:8787 while testing against a local server.

            Prefer leaving this blank and setting it on the setup page instead:
            the address belongs to the machine running the console, which moves
            whenever it joins another network, and a value baked into the
            firmware would have to be reflashed to follow it.  Whatever is set
            here is only the fallback used when the setup page supplied nothing.

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_KEY
        string "Device key"
        depends on ROBOT_CLOUD_ENABLE
        default ""
        help
            Short code issued once when binding the device in the console.

            Best left blank: enter it on the setup page instead, so the firmware
            image carries no credential at all.  Anything set here is compiled
            into the binary, so an sdkconfig holding a real key must never be
            committed.  As with the URL, this value is only the fallback used
            when the setup page supplied nothing.

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_POLL_INTERVAL_S
        int "Command poll interval (seconds)"
        depends on ROBOT_CLOUD_ENABLE
        default 3
        range 1 60

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_STATUS_INTERVAL_S
        int "Status report interval (seconds)"
        depends on ROBOT_CLOUD_ENABLE
        default 60
        range 10 3600

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_VOICE
        bool "Enable the cloud voice conversation channel"
        depends on ROBOT_CLOUD_ENABLE
        default n
        help
            When enabled the device can record one utterance, send it to the
            console server and play back the spoken answer.  The server runs
            speech recognition, the model call and speech synthesis, so the
            account needs voice credentials configured in the console and the
            device needs a voice role selected there.

            Requires the account to have configured voice credentials and this
            device to have a voice role, otherwise the server answers 409 and
            the robot reports the reason without recording anything.

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_VOICE_MAX_RECORD_MS
        int "Recording ceiling per utterance (milliseconds)"
        depends on ROBOT_CLOUD_VOICE
        default 10000
        range 1000 30000
        help
            Hard stop for one utterance.  A speech that long is unusual, so
            this mostly bounds how much PSRAM the recording buffer reserves.

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_VOICE_SILENCE_MS
        int "Trailing silence that ends an utterance (milliseconds)"
        depends on ROBOT_CLOUD_VOICE
        default 900
        range 300 3000
        help
            How long the robot waits after you stop talking before it sends
            the audio.  Lower feels snappier but clips slow speakers.

    config ROBOT_CLOUD_VOICE_THRESHOLD
        int "Silence threshold (16-bit PCM level)"
        depends on ROBOT_CLOUD_VOICE
        default 700
        range 40 4000
        help
            Frames whose RMS is below this count as silence; after
            ROBOT_CLOUD_VOICE_SILENCE_MS of them the utterance is sent.  Raise it
            in a noisy room, lower it if the robot stops before you finish.

            This number is only meaningful next to the capture gain, because it
            is an absolute level: change ROBOT_MIC_SHIFT and it has to be moved
            by the same factor.  It used to sit at 320, chosen while the gain
            was 16x too high (see ROBOT_MIC_SHIFT); once that was corrected,
            ordinary speech reported an RMS of 1500-2300 while the threshold
            stayed at roughly a fifth of it, so room noise tripped it constantly
            and the utterance always ran to the recording ceiling.  Measured on
            the DeskEmoji board with the gain corrected: 700 ends the utterance
            about a second after you stop talking.

endmenu
